<h2 id="gradients">
Gradients</h2>

	A gradient is an image that smoothly fades from one color to another.
	These are commonly used for subtle shading in background images, buttons, and many other things.
	The <dfn export lt="gradient function">gradient functions</dfn> described in this section allow an author to specify such an image in a terse syntax,
	so that the UA can generate the image automatically when rendering the page.
	The syntax of a <<gradient>> is:

	<pre class=prod>
		<dfn>&lt;gradient></dfn> = [
			<<linear-gradient()>> | <<repeating-linear-gradient()>> |
			<<radial-gradient()>> | <<repeating-radial-gradient()>> |
			<<conic-gradient()>>  | <<repeating-conic-gradient()>> |
			<<mesh-gradient()>> | <<freeform-gradient()>> ]
	</pre>

	<div class='example' id='ex-gradients-are-images'>

		As with the other <<image>> types defined in this specification,
		gradients can be used in any property that accepts images.
		For example:

		<ul class=lang-css>
			<li><code>background: linear-gradient(white, gray);</code>
			<li><code>list-style-image: radial-gradient(circle, #006, #00a 90%, #0000af 100%, white 100%)</code>
		</ul>
	</div>

	A gradient is drawn into a box with the dimensions of the <a>concrete object size</a>,
	referred to as the <dfn export>gradient box</dfn>.
	However, the gradient itself has no [=natural dimensions=].

	<wpt>
		gradients-with-border.html
	</wpt>

	<div class='example' id='ex-default-gradient-box'>
		For example, if you use a gradient as a background,
		by default the gradient will draw into a <a>gradient box</a> the size of the element's padding box.
		If 'background-size' is explicitly set to a value such as ''100px 200px'',
		then the <a>gradient box</a> will be 100px wide and 200px tall.
		Similarly, for a gradient used as a 'list-style-image',
		the box would be a 1em square,
		which is the <a>default object size</a> for that property.
	</div>

	Gradients are either defined along one dimension (1D), like linear and conic gradients,
	or along two dimensions (2D), like mesh and freeform gradients.

	1D gradients are specified by defining the <dfn>starting point</dfn> and <dfn>ending point</dfn>
	of a <dfn export>gradient line</dfn>
	(which, depending on the type of gradient,
	may be technically a line, or a ray, or a spiral),
	and then specifying colors at points along this line.
	The colors are smoothly blended to fill in the rest of the line,
	and then each type of 1D gradient defines how to use the color of the <a>gradient line</a> to produce the actual gradient.

	2D gradients are specified by defining a set of points on a two dimensional space,
	each with an associated color,
	and then smoothly blending the colors
	to produce gradients between them.
	Each type of 2D gradient defines how to position the points
	and how the gradient is produced.

Mesh Gradients: the ''mesh-gradient()'' notation {#mesh-gradients}
------------------------------------------------------------------------

	A mesh gradient is a 2D gradient
	created by specifying a two dimensional grid-like mesh,
	with a color defined at each vertex on the mesh.
	Each cell in the grid-like mesh is known as a <i>patch</i>
	and the colors at the four vertices (i.e. corners) of the patch are interpolated
	to produce a smooth gradient.

	<div class='example' id='ex-mesh-gradient-visualization'>
		This example creates a mesh
		that has three mesh points per row and three rows in total:

		<pre class="lang-css">
			background-image: mesh-gradient(
				red,  blue, red;
				blue, red,  blue;
				red,  blue, red
			);
		</pre>

		The lines of the mesh are illustrated in the following image.

		<img alt="There are three horizontal and three vertical lines going through the mesh."
		     src="images/mesh-gradient-grid.png"
			 width="372" height="366">
	</div>

	Note: Be careful when using a grid metaphor to describe a mesh.
	Typically when choosing number of rows and columns a grid has,
	you describe the number of cells in a row or column.
	However, when specifying the size of a mesh,
	you are describing the number of vertices in a row or column
	(i.e. the points where the mesh lines intersect).

	The grid can have any number of rows and columns.
	Each vertex can be positioned anywhere on the grid
	and the edges can be curved with control points.

<h4 class='no-toc' id='mesh-gradient-syntax'>
mesh-gradient() Syntax</h4>

	The mesh points are specified as a grid.
	Each <<mesh-point>> on a row is separated by a comma.
	A semicolon starts a new row.

	All rows should have an equal number of mesh points on them.
	If there is an inconsistent number of mesh points specified in the rows,
	the function is invalid.

	Issue: When there is an inconsistent number of mesh points in each row,
	should we define a fallback
	rather than making the function invalid?

	The syntax for a mesh gradient is:

	<pre class='prod'>
		<dfn>mesh-gradient()</dfn> = mesh-gradient( [ <<mesh-gradient-syntax>> ] )
		<dfn>&lt;mesh-gradient-syntax></dfn> =
			[ <<color-interpolation-method>> || <<color>> ]?, <<mesh-point>># [ ';' <<mesh-point>># ]*

		<dfn>&lt;mesh-point></dfn> = <<color>> [ <<mesh-absolute-position>> | <<mesh-relative-position>> ]?
		<dfn>&lt;mesh-absolute-position></dfn> = <<position>> [ with <<position>> [ / <<position>> ]{0,3} ]?
		<dfn>&lt;mesh-relative-position></dfn> = by <<coordinate-pair>> [ with <<mesh-relative-control-point>> [ / <<mesh-relative-control-point>> ]{0,3} ]?
		<dfn>&lt;mesh-relative-control-point></dfn> = <<coordinate-pair>> [from origin]?
	</pre>

	The arguments are defined as follows:

	<dl dfn-type=value dfn-for="mesh-gradient()">
		<dt><dfn><<coordinate-pair>></dfn> = <<length-percentage>>{2}
		<dd>Defines a pair of coordinates,
			representing a rightward and downward offset, respectively,
			from a specified reference point.
			Percentages are resolved against the width or height, respectively,
			of the [=gradient box=].

		<dt><dfn><<mesh-point>></dfn> = <<color>> [ <<mesh-absolute-position>> | <<mesh-relative-position>> ]?
		<dd>
			A vertex on the grid.
	</dl>

	TODO

	Issue: TODO: copy shape() for some arguments definitions

	<div class="issue">
		Tab's explanation:

		* `&lt;color-interpolation-method>` is self-explanatory. The `&lt;color>` defines the
			color "outside" of the mesh, only visible if your outermost points aren't on the
			edges of the image; defaulting to `transparent`. (It's not painted "under" the
			gradient, just "around" it; a mesh with partially-transparent colors will still
			be transparent even if you specify a color here.)
		* The body of the function is comma-separated sequences of mesh points defining
			a row, with each row separated by a `;`. (Using `;` rather than `/` because I'm
			borrowing from `shape()` elsewhere and that uses `/`.) Each row needs to have
			the same number of points, or the function is invalid. Each mesh point has:
			*  a `&lt;color>`
			* positions with optional control points. Cribbing from shape(): if
				specified as a `&lt;position>`, it and its control points are all absolute
				coords in the mesh's coordinate space. If given as `by ...`, it and its
				control points are relative to the trivial NxM even grid position.
				* The outer points don't use all four of the control points, but they're
					still specified in the grammar; you can supply `0 0` as an easy null value
					for the unused ones.
			* An omitted control point takes from the value of another one, as usual for
				CSS's "1-4 values in TRBL order" rules, except we rotate them into position
				around the point. This gives the desired default behavior where, if you
				specify just two, you'll get the correct control points on the other side to
				keep your color ramp smooth. (In Illustrator, opposite-side control points
				are locked to be half-turn rotations from each other by default, unless you
				purposely unlock them.)
	</div>

	Note: Authors are free to define patches that overlap each other,
	but that can lead to unexpected results.

